<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/create_buffer_pool.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="sql-createbufferpool">
 <indexterm zone="sql-createbufferpool">
  <primary>CREATE BUFFER POOL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>CREATE BUFFER POOL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>CREATE BUFFER POOL</refname>
  <refpurpose>define a new buffer pool</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
CREATE BUFFER POOL <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> HANDLER <replaceable class="parameter">handler_name</replaceable> SIZE '<replaceable class="parameter">size</replaceable>'
    [ WITH ( <replaceable class="parameter">option</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ... ] ) ]
CREATE BUFFER POOL <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable> REMAINDER HANDLER <replaceable class="parameter">handler_name</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE BUFFER POOL</command> registers a new, named buffer pool.
   A buffer pool is a separately managed region of shared cache memory with
   its own page-replacement algorithm.  Relations can be directed to a
   specific pool with the <literal>buffer_pool</literal> storage parameter
   (see <xref linkend="sql-createtable"/>), which lets workloads with
   different access patterns be isolated from one another and from the
   cluster's default clock-sweep cache.
  </para>

  <para>
   The named pool is created in addition to the always-present
   <literal>default</literal> pool, which owns
   <xref linkend="guc-shared-buffers"/>.  A pool created with an explicit
   <literal>SIZE</literal> is a <firstterm>dynamic</firstterm> pool: its
   memory is allocated from dynamic shared memory when the pool is created,
   over and above <varname>shared_buffers</varname>.  A pool created with the
   <literal>REMAINDER</literal> clause instead takes over the portion of
   <varname>shared_buffers</varname> that is not claimed by any other
   user-created dynamic pool; only one <literal>REMAINDER</literal> pool may
   exist at a time.
  </para>

  <para>
   The page-replacement algorithm is provided by the handler function named
   in the <literal>HANDLER</literal> clause.  The built-in handlers are
   <function>clock_pool_handler</function> (clock-sweep, the same algorithm
   used by the default pool), <function>keep_pool_handler</function> (never
   evicts), and <function>jam_pool_handler</function> (a write-optimized
   strategy intended for <acronym>TOAST</acronym>/overflow data).  Additional
   handlers can be registered by extensions loaded through
   <xref linkend="guc-shared-preload-libraries"/>.
  </para>

  <para>
   Only superusers can create buffer pools.  A buffer pool cannot be created
   during recovery.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of the buffer pool to be created.  The name must be distinct
      from the name of any existing buffer pool, including the
      <literal>default</literal> pool.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">handler_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name of a previously registered handler function that implements the
      pool's page-replacement algorithm.  The handler function must take a
      single argument of type <type>internal</type> and return
      <type>internal</type>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">size</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The size of the pool, in bytes.  The value is given as a quoted string
      of decimal digits.  The size must be positive and must correspond to at
      least 16 buffers (that is, 16 &times; <symbol>BLCKSZ</symbol> bytes,
      typically 128&nbsp;kB).  This memory is allocated in addition to
      <varname>shared_buffers</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>REMAINDER</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Instead of an explicit size, size the pool automatically to the portion
      of <varname>shared_buffers</varname> that is not claimed by any other
      user-created dynamic pool.  A <literal>NOTICE</literal> reports the
      resulting number of buffers.  Only one <literal>REMAINDER</literal> pool
      is allowed at a time, and it cannot be created while any user pool is
      oversubscribed (that is, temporarily larger than its target size while
      the trickle writer drains it) or if no unclaimed space remains.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>WITH ( <replaceable class="parameter">option</replaceable> [= <replaceable class="parameter">value</replaceable>] [, ... ] )</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      This clause specifies optional parameters for the buffer pool.  The
      following options are recognized:

      <variablelist>
       <varlistentry>
        <term><literal>huge_pages</literal> (<type>boolean</type>)</term>
        <listitem>
         <para>
          Whether to request huge pages for the pool's memory.  The default is
          <literal>false</literal>.  As with <xref linkend="guc-huge-pages"/>,
          huge page support is platform-dependent.
         </para>
        </listitem>
       </varlistentry>

       <varlistentry>
        <term><literal>direct_io</literal> (<type>boolean</type>)</term>
        <listitem>
         <para>
          Whether the pool should use direct I/O, bypassing the operating
          system page cache, for its own reads and writes.  The default is
          <literal>false</literal>.  This option can also be changed later with
          <xref linkend="sql-alterbufferpool"/>.
         </para>
        </listitem>
       </varlistentry>
      </variablelist></para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A buffer pool caches on-disk data; dropping, resizing, or otherwise
   recreating a pool discards its cached pages but never affects the data on
   disk.
  </para>

  <para>
   A relation cannot be assigned to a pool that uses direct I/O in a way that
   conflicts with the cluster's I/O configuration; see
   <xref linkend="guc-max-buffer-pool-memory"/> for the address-space
   reservation that same-address pools, asynchronous I/O on pool buffers, and
   online resize require.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   Create a 256&nbsp;MB clock-sweep pool and direct a table to it:
<programlisting>
CREATE BUFFER POOL analytics HANDLER clock_pool_handler SIZE '268435456';
CREATE TABLE events (...) WITH (buffer_pool = 'analytics');
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Create a write-optimized pool for a table's <acronym>TOAST</acronym> data:
<programlisting>
CREATE BUFFER POOL toast_pool HANDLER jam_pool_handler SIZE '134217728';
CREATE TABLE documents (id int, body text)
    WITH (overflow_buffer_pool = 'toast_pool');
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Carve a pool out of the remaining unclaimed portion of
   <varname>shared_buffers</varname>:
<programlisting>
CREATE BUFFER POOL leftover REMAINDER HANDLER clock_pool_handler;
</programlisting></para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>CREATE BUFFER POOL</command> is a
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> extension.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>See Also</title>

